    "text": "In the given figure magnetic field at the center of ring&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>8</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math>. Now it is turned through&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>90</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>xx</mi><mo>'</mo></math> axis, so that two semicircular parts are mutually perpendicular. The new value of magnetic field (in Tesla) at centre is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></math>. Find <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math>.<br />\r<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0115_805833234e5d.jpg\" style=\"width: 500px; height: 200px;\" />"
  },
  "options": [],
